{
 "id": "hyaline_cartilage",
 "title": "Inside hyaline cartilage",
 "node": "hc_scene",
 "bg": "#f3efe6",
 "center": [
  0,
  0,
  0
 ],
 "D_um": 800,
 "tag": "Inside cartilage",
 "cell": "animal",
 "blurb": "A slice of glassy (hyaline) cartilage, as in the windpipe rings, ribs and nose. Cells sit alone or in small groups inside spaces in a matrix with no blood vessels, under a skin of connective tissue at the top. Click a cell, a group, the matrix or the skin.",
 "note": "Schematic, not a micrograph. Real: the size of one cartilage cell with its surrounding space (chondron) and how many sit in a cubic millimetre, from 3D X-ray imaging of healthy human knee cartilage (about 3,500 to 6,400 cubic micrometres for a single one, 6,600 to 13,600 for a cluster, 3,400 to 5,100 per cubic millimetre, by depth zone); the shapes drawn are ellipsoids of exactly those volumes, with axis ratios that are illustrative (flatter near the edge). Also real: joint-surface cartilage has no blood vessels (not sourced for rib cartilage), nutrients reach the cells through the matrix, and joint-surface cartilage has no perichondrium. Illustrative: the 600 micrometre window, the 40 micrometre perichondrium, its fibroblasts, fibres and capillaries, the cell inside each space (three quarters of its width), 2 to 4 cells per group, the share of cells in groups (one in five), the positions, and all colours. The three depth bands stand in for the three zones of joint-surface cartilage, which spans 2 to 4 mm, so this window is a thin slice of one kind, not a whole joint surface; the perichondrium belongs to windpipe, rib and nose cartilage. The capillary uses the vessel_wall material, which on zooming in shows a larger vessel wall with smooth muscle; a real capillary wall is a single lining cell layer.",
 "labels": [
  {
   "text": "Perichondrium (the skin of the cartilage, with blood vessels)",
   "at": [
    -300,
    390,
    50
   ],
   "pop": "peri"
  },
